WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:04.100
We sifted through the list of upcoming shows
and tried to determine which ones might not

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.800
survive 2019.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.320
Maybe it's a case of a bad premise, showrunners
with awful track records, or just tired storylines.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.960
Regardless, this group of programs may not
be long for the televised world.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.780
ABC is no stranger to genre thrillers based
around supernatural events or characters that

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.740
have otherworldly powers.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.580
In that vein, the Alphabet Network is bringing
us Emergence, about a police chief who finds

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.260
a mysterious young girl near the site of a
major accident.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.920
That discovery leads to a vast conspiracy
surrounding the child.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.120
"I found a kid at the crash site I'm being
told wasn't even on the plane."

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.000
Though the idea of superhuman kids on television
is far from new, networks haven't had the

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.280
best success with the concept in recent years.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.620
Fox dipped its toes into the Marvel universe
in 2017 with The Gifted, a show about a family

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.760
that discovers its children have the mutant
gene.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.200
The show lasted two seasons before being canceled.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.370
The network tried again two years later with
The Passage, in which a federal agent protects

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.400
a young girl at the center of a series of
top-secret government tests.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.900
That show lasted ten episodes before getting
the ax.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.200
And when you realize that ABC has struggled
with some of its other out-of-this-world sci-fi

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.160
shows like FlashForward or V, it seems Emergence
could be headed for a relatively quick disappearance.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.700
ABC has been riding a hot hand in recent years
with the works of Shonda Rhimes.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.340
From Grey's Anatomy, to Scandal, to How to
Get Away With Murder, Shondaland is a world

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.640
of televised delights.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.580
They're also all major hits.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.820
Now, ABC is hoping to replicate the same magic
with showrunner Kenya Barris, whose Black-ish

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.400
scored well enough on the parent network to
earn a spin-off, Grown-ish, that airs on the

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.660
Disney-owned cable channel Freeform.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.200
Now comes Mixed-ish, a prequel of sorts to
Black-ish.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.480
The series follows Rainbow Johnson as she
recalls her childhood growing up in the 1980s

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.480
and the struggles of being a mixed-race child
in a world that can't decide where she should

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:50.940
fit in.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.760
But unlike Shonda Rhimes' shows, which all
have vastly different premises and storylines,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.120
Mixed-Ish seems kind of like a third version
of the same story.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.600
It's hard to imagine that viewers are interested
enough in the Johnson family to want a third

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.270
series about them, especially one that seems
to simply cover old ground.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.340
"The world smacked you in the face today,
didn't it.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.220
You can tell me."

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.380
Fox is hoping for a winner with its family
sitcom Outmatched.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.129
The show stars Jason Biggs and Maggie Lawson
as a working-class New Jersey family with

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:19.740
four kids.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.260
Nothing remarkable there — except three
of those kids happen to be geniuses.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.280
"I will not be a Salieri in my own home!"

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.820
"I don't know what any of that means!"

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.060
Television has a storied history of giving
us precocious children in sitcoms.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.280
It's what helped make Joey Lawrence, the Olsen
twins, and the kids from Modern Family household

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:37.800
names.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.960
But it's a fine line that shows walk with
young characters.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.860
Everyone hopes to be Stewie Griffin.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.340
Few want to be Steve Urkel.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.360
The idea of smart-alecky, supergenius kids
feels like an immediate turnoff — unless

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.680
you're a spinoff of The Big Bang Theory.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.159
Stop us if you've heard this one: A hoity-toity
type from a big city finds himself in a small

00:02:56.159 --> 00:03:00.540
town where he has to absorb the culture shock
and find a way to assimilate — or at least

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.940
tolerate — a completely new way of doing
things.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.180
This premise has worked to great success in
a number of shows.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.060
Now, NBC is hoping to find similar success
with Perfect Harmony.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.820
The single-camera sitcom stars Bradley Whitford,
who's in the midst of a run on the highly-regarded

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.680
The Handmaid's Tale.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.140
He also previously found big success on the
Peacock Network as Josh Lyman on The West

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:20.680
Wing.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.260
This time around, Whitford is a former Ivy
League music professor who finds himself working

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.100
with the choir of a small-town church.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.140
As you'd expect, there's a major clash of
sensibilities between Arthur Cochran and his

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.180
new musical charges.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.920
Behind the scenes, the hope is that Lesley
Wake Webster and Jason Winer — two of the

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.439
forces behind CBS's Life in Pieces — can
make the show a hit, but it will be a challenge

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.820
to see how they can make this pretty specific
take on an old premise stick with TV audiences.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:51.540
No one could ever accuse CBS of not giving
their audiences what they want.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.640
If there's one thing the Tiffany Network is
chock full of, it's crime and courtroom dramas.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:01.540
From the CSI and NCIS franchises to Bull and
The Good Fight, CBS viewers probably have

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.900
a good idea of how scripted, dramatic jurisprudence
works.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.120
Yet for those television watchers tired of
seeing shows about the inner workings of the

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.340
legal system, well, here's another show about
the inner workings of the legal system.

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:18.360
All Rise follows the day-to-day challenges
of judges and lawyers in Los Angeles as they

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.810
try to work within an inadequate system attempting
to deliver justice, all while trying to make

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.560
sense of their own lives in the process.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.740
It's one thing to find a winning formula and
attempt to duplicate it.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.080
It's another to continually reheat and rearrange
the same ingredients and try to call it a

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:33.600
new dish.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.760
CBS recently canceled The Code after just
one season when that courtroom drama that

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.090
failed to gain any traction.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.600
Will All Rise stand up and distinguish itself?

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.400
Or will it follow the same fate?

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:49.340
It's not uncommon for American television
producers to look overseas to find inspiration

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.379
for domestic shows.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:52.980
Sometimes it works, and sometimes it doesn't.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.940
Israeli television seems to have created a
hit with The Baker and the Beauty, a show

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.620
about a humble baker who falls in love and
begins dating an international supermodel.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.300
The series was popular enough that Amazon
picked up the show's first two seasons for

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.620
international streaming.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:12.060
Around the same time, the show was reportedly
being adapted by Universal Television for

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.840
American audiences and eventually ordered
to series for the fall season.

00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:20.180
In the American version, the setting moves
to Miami, with the main characters being of

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:20.720
Cuban descent.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.370
It's another in a string of ABC shows featuring
Latinx actors and characters, similar to Ugly

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:27.640
Betty and Grand Hotel.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.340
Yet while those other shows featured familiar
faces and star power to potentially bring

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.199
viewers, The Baker and the Beauty stars a
number of actors who might not be well-known

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.360
by a wide audience.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.080
Can the power of this unlikely love story
be enough to attract watchers, or is the American

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.420
version of this affair, supposedly debuting
midseason, doomed to go up in smoke?

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:52.290
In the '90s, Fox scored big with The X-Files,
a series about a science-minded FBI agent

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:56.789
paired with a wild-eyed believer in conspiracy
theories as they chased fantastical cases

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:58.300
with a supernatural tinge.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:03.420
While you couldn't exactly call Fox Mulder
a religious zealot, his steadfast faith in

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.669
forces greater than mankind made for a compelling
back-and-forth with the more staid and methodical

00:06:07.669 --> 00:06:08.220
Dana Scully.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.380
With Evil, CBS is attempting to resurrect
a similar dynamic.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.740
The show centers on a questioning female clinical
psychologist who teams up with a priest-in-training

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.060
and a blue-collar carpenter to probe a backlog
of the Church's unexplained phenomena.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:24.600
"I know you believe in this stuff.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.080
I don't."

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.540
If the premise feels more than a little X-Files-y
to you, you're probably not alone.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:34.120
The difference is that the CBS iteration is
likely to have many more Biblical references

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.499
than its paranormal predecessor.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.240
In and of itself, that's not a bad thing — the
network had a long-running hit with Touched

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.000
by an Angel, and it's done fairly well with
the series God Friended Me.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:47.320
But it's worth wondering if there will be
an audience for a show that pits science versus

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.780
religion in such a dramatic way, without a
clear point of view to draw audiences in.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.120
Religious beliefs tend to run a lot deeper
than beliefs in UFOs, after all.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.680
If the show tries to pay equal lip service
to a doubter and a true believer, then it's

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.820
fair to wonder who the show will really be
for.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.000
Audiences might not find the show to be evil
itself, but they might not think it's all

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.040
that good, either.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:12.900
Networks are keen on building sitcoms around
popular comedians, whether up-and-coming funny

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.280
people or venerable entertainers that audiences
know after watching them for years.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.600
Kenan Thompson falls squarely in the latter
category.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.550
The veteran funnyman broke onto the scene
as a child actor in multiple Nickelodeon shows

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:28.600
before beginning his run on Saturday Night
Live in 2003.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.120
He currently holds the record as the show's
longest-tenured cast member.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.380
Some of the more successful series built around
comedians draw from the performer's work,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.200
life, or personality.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.780
Some of the worst tend to stifle an actor's
creativity in an effort to put them in a network-friendly

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:43.320
box.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:47.080
NBC hopes to avoid that second category with
The Kenan Show.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.620
On the show, Thompson will play a widowed
father of two dealing with an overbearing

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:53.660
father-in-law — a familiar premise, at least.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.920
Nothing about the concept seems particularly
awful.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.240
But nothing about it seems all that compelling,
either.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.720
The idea of the harried single dad trying
to balance work and home life has been done

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.720
plenty of times, and it hasn't felt particularly
fresh since… maybe My Three Sons.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:13.780
Part of what's made Thompson a stalwart on
SNL for more than a decade-and-a-half has

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.600
been his ability to play hilarious and outrageous
characters.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.580
Now it seems like NBC is asking him to play
a much more sedate persona in a format that's

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.320
been done to death.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.500
The show's not even out yet, but it kind of
already feels like a relic of the past.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:31.939
The idea that robots or other forms of artificial
intelligence will rise up and eventually signal

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:35.860
the destruction of mankind is an idea as old
as science fiction itself.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:39.740
Who hasn't seen the video of robots doing
parkour and wondered if the nightmares they've

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:43.360
had about their own cyber-based demise were
actually on the verge of coming true?

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.210
Who hasn't stood on a street corner, screaming
that Skynet had become self-aware, and that

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:49.640
Terminators were coming for us all?

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:51.340
Just us?

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:52.180
Okay.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.960
Anyway, Fox is hoping that you haven't tired
of stories of dystopian digital futures with

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.120
its new show, neXt.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.100
The futuristic thriller stars John Slattery
as a tech entrepreneur who realizes that an

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.940
artificial intelligence application he created
could eventually lead to the world's destruction.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.320
In an era where we already have smart homes,
our phones are always listening to us, and

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.410
companies and governments are doing who knows
what with the data, it might be hard to get

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.760
viewers too worked up over the premise of
the show.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.790
Seriously, have you kept up with the news
on this stuff?

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:23.740
This tech dystopia looks too familiar.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.540
We want to see something with robots.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:29.120
A son is being asked to follow in his father's
footsteps.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.540
The son rebels against being pigeonholed into
a career that he doesn't want.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.200
Reluctantly, that son eventually succumbs
to his destiny, though it might be in a different

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:38.680
way than was originally envisioned.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.420
Many an on-screen tale has told that story.

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:45.820
But this fall when you tune into Fox's Prodigal
Son, you'll be asked to consider … what

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.640
if the family business was murder?

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:52.940
"Malcolm… my boy.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.500
On the show, Tom Payne plays the son of a
convicted serial killer asked to track down

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:58.680
other serial killers.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.800
Imagine if Dexter's son eventually became
a homicide detective.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.780
The show is expected to have elements of dark
comedy, which might work in its favor.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:11.120
Otherwise, this could end up as another in
a long line of homicidal maniac-themed mystery

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:16.480
shows that get bogged down in a premise that
relies too heavily on a weird father-son relationship

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:18.860
and not enough about catching the bad guys.

